# 🌊 Solana Security Guide

## Overview

Solana programs (smart contracts) are written in Rust, typically using the Anchor framework. Key differences from EVM:
- **Account-based model** vs storage model
- **Program Derived Addresses (PDAs)** for deterministic addressing
- **Cross-Program Invocation (CPI)** for composability
- **Parallel transaction execution**

---

## 1. Missing Signer Checks

```rust
// VULNERABLE - no signer verification
pub fn update_admin(ctx: Context<UpdateAdmin>, new_admin: Pubkey) -> Result<()> {
    ctx.accounts.config.admin = new_admin;  // Anyone can call!
    Ok(())
}

#[derive(Accounts)]
pub struct UpdateAdmin<'info> {
    #[account(mut)]
    pub config: Account<'info, Config>,
    // Missing: authority check
}

// SECURE - verify signer
#[derive(Accounts)]
pub struct UpdateAdmin<'info> {
    #[account(mut, has_one = admin)]
    pub config: Account<'info, Config>,
    #[account(signer)]  // Must sign transaction
    pub admin: Signer<'info>,
}

// Alternative: Manual check in native Rust (without Anchor)
if !ctx.accounts.authority.is_signer {
    return Err(ProgramError::MissingRequiredSignature.into());
}
```

